  "prediction": "The Department of Defense (DOD) has policies and procedures in place to prevent and respond to unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. However, the report highlights several areas for improvement.\n\nThe report notes that DOD's policies on sexual harassment do not fully include principles from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's (CDC) framework for preventing sexual violence. Specifically, the policies lack risk and protective factors, risk domains, and tertiary strategies for prevention. The report also finds that DOD's policies on sexual harassment do not consistently include mechanisms for anonymous reporting, as required by the National Defense Authorization Act for FY 2013.\n\nThe report also examines DOD's data collection efforts on unwanted sexual behaviors. While DOD has reliable data systems for collecting and reporting data on sexual assault and domestic violence involving sexual assault, inconsistencies in data elements and definitions may be creating difficulties in developing department-wide reports on unwanted sexual behaviors. The report recommends that DOD develop standard data elements and definitions for maintaining and reporting information on sexual harassment incidents and direct the military services to incorporate these into their databases.\n\nIn addition, the report notes that DOD has several overarching efforts to address unwanted sexual behaviors across the continuum of harm, including the establishment of an office to oversee the integration and coordination of unwanted sexual behaviors and the development of an overarching prevention strategy. However, the report finds that DOD's prevention strategy lacks key elements, such as a mission statement, long-term goals, and strategies to achieve goals.\n\nThe report makes four recommendations to DOD:\n\n1. Fully include in the new policy for sexual harassment the principles in the CDC's framework for sexual violence prevention, including risk and protective factors, risk domains, and tertiary strategies.\n2. Include in the new policy for sexual harassment mechanisms for anonymous reporting of incidents consistent with section 579 of the National Defense Authorization Act for FY 2013.\n3. Direct the Office of Diversity Management and Equal Opportunity to develop standard data elements and definitions for maintaining and reporting information on sexual harassment incidents at the military service level, and direct the military services to incorporate these data elements and definitions into their military service-specific databases.\n4. Direct the Assistant Secretary of Defense for Readiness to incorporate in its continuum of harm prevention strategy all the elements that are key for establishing a long-term, results-oriented strategic planning framework.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for DOD to improve its policies and procedures for preventing and responding to unwanted sexual behaviors, as well as its data collection efforts and prevention strategy.",
